  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will bring lots of dry weather, \ncoastal breezes, and near to below normal temperatures through mid \nnext week.\n\nPersistent high pressure will dominate the weather through mid next \nweek leading to dry conditions and generally near to below normal \ntemperatures. A few showers could brush the coast at times but no \nsignificant rainfall is expected. Breezy conditions with gusts up to \naround 35 mph at times are also expected, especially near the coast, \ndue to low pressure well to the southeast from near The Bahamas \nnortheast to Bermuda. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor coastal flooding and hazardous marine/surf \nconditions are expected through at least early next week. \n\nAn extended period of enhanced northeast winds due to strong high \npressure to the north and low pressure developing near The Bahamas \nwill lead to hazardous marine/surf conditions. Also, increasing \nastronomical tides and NE winds will lead to minor coastal flooding.\n\nCoastal Flooding: Minor coastal flooding is expected during each\nhigh tide cycle for the next several days along the SE NC and \nNE SC coasts, including the lower Cape Fear River. The highest\ntides should occur during the morning high tide cycles.\n\nHigh Surf: High seas up to near 10 ft within 20 nm will likely lead \nto 6+ ft surf heights into early next week, especially for E-facing \nbeaches. We have raised a High Surf Advisory for New Hanover County \nwhere confidence is highest in 6 ft breakers occurring starting\ntoday. In addition, the large waves coupled with high water \nlevels should result in some erosion, especially for E-facing \nbeaches.\n\nMarine: See the Marine section below for details regarding marine \nhazards.",
